-- Q740: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When the tax return is filed with the collecting jurisdiction","When the underlying exchange transaction occurs and resources are available","When the government includes the revenue in its approved annual budget","When cash is physically received by the government treasury only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 33, paragraph 16, derived tax revenues such as sales taxes are recognized when the underlying exchange transaction occurs (the retail sale) and resources are available, meaning collected within the availability period (typically 60 days after fiscal year-end for modified accrual).
Wrong (A): The filing of a tax return is not the recognition trigger. Recognition depends on when the underlying taxable transaction occurs, not administrative filing dates.
Wrong (C): Budgeting revenue does not trigger recognition. Revenue must meet the measurable and available criteria regardless of budget assumptions.
Wrong (D): Cash receipt alone is not the criterion. Modified accrual recognizes revenue when the underlying transaction occurs and resources are available within the availability period, which may precede actual cash receipt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 740;

-- Q1066: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both stages are capitalized because all costs necessary for the project are capitalizable","Preliminary stage costs are expensed; application development costs are capitalized per ASC 350-40","Both stages are expensed because internal-use software costs are never capitalizable under GAAP","Vendor evaluation is capitalized while coding costs are expensed as period costs"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 350-40 defines three stages for internal-use software. Preliminary project stage costs (vendor evaluation, feasibility analysis) are expensed because the outcome is uncertain, similar to research under ASC 730. Application development stage costs (coding, testing) are capitalized once management commits to the project and future economic benefit is probable.
Wrong (A): This ignores the stage-based framework. Costs during the preliminary stage lack the certainty threshold for capitalization.
Wrong (C): ASC 350-40 explicitly allows capitalization of application development stage costs for internal-use software.
Wrong (D): This reverses the correct treatment. Vendor evaluation is a preliminary stage cost that is expensed, while coding is an application development cost that is capitalized.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1066;

-- Q1070: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both arrangements are treated identically because cloud costs are always expensed regardless of classification","CCA-1 records a software asset while CCA-2 records an operating expense since hosted arrangements provide more control","Neither arrangement affects the balance sheet because all cloud costs are period expenses under current GAAP","CCA-1 capitalizes implementation costs as a prepaid asset; CCA-2 records a software intangible under ASC 350-40"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASU 2018-15, CCA-2 (software license) gives the customer control of the software, so the $500,000 is capitalized as an intangible asset under ASC 350-40. CCA-1 (service contract) does not transfer control, so qualifying implementation costs are capitalized as a prepaid asset and amortized over the service term. Cash flows differ as well: investing for CCA-2, operating for CCA-1.
Wrong (A): Both ASC 350-40 and ASU 2018-15 provide capitalization provisions for qualifying cloud computing costs.
Wrong (B): This reverses the classifications. The software license (CCA-2) creates the software asset, not the service contract.
Wrong (C): Both arrangements can create balance sheet assets, though the nature of the asset differs between a software intangible and a prepaid expense.$EXPL$
