-- Q1071: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All $120,000 is capitalized because any cloud implementation cost qualifies for capitalization","The $50,000 coding qualifies for capitalization; $45,000 migration and $25,000 training are expensed","Only the $25,000 training cost is capitalizable as the most valuable SaaS implementation investment","All $120,000 must be expensed because SaaS service contracts prohibit any cost capitalization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): ASU 2018-15 applies ASC 350-40''s stage framework to SaaS service contracts. The $50,000 custom interface coding is an application development activity that qualifies for capitalization as a prepaid asset. Data migration ($45,000) is a preliminary or data conversion cost that is expensed, and training ($25,000) is a post-implementation cost explicitly excluded from capitalization.
Wrong (A): Not all implementation spending qualifies. The stage-based framework distinguishes capitalizable development costs from non-capitalizable preliminary and post-implementation costs.
Wrong (C): Training is never capitalizable under ASC 350-40 or ASU 2018-15. It is a post-implementation cost expensed as incurred.
Wrong (D): ASU 2018-15 specifically permits capitalization of qualifying implementation costs for SaaS service contracts during the application development stage.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1071;

-- Q1072: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Prior years'' amortization is restated to reflect the 3-year life, increasing accumulated amortization retroactively","The method switches from straight-line to accelerated amortization to catch up on underreported expense","The remaining $360,000 book value is amortized over the remaining 1 year as a prospective estimate change","No adjustment is needed because the original 5-year estimate is locked in at the capitalization date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 250, a change in useful life is a change in accounting estimate handled prospectively. The remaining book value of $360,000 ($600,000 minus $240,000 accumulated) is amortized over the revised remaining life of 1 year (3-year total minus 2 years elapsed), producing $360,000 of amortization in Year 3. This is not a change in accounting principle.
Wrong (A): Retrospective restatement applies to changes in accounting principle, not changes in estimate. Useful life revisions are prospective under ASC 250.
Wrong (B): Changing the useful life does not require changing the amortization method. Straight-line continues; only the remaining period changes.
Wrong (D): Useful life estimates are explicitly subject to revision as circumstances change. ASC 250 requires updating estimates when new information becomes available.$EXPL$
