-- Q1076: Advanced Lease Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both scenarios qualify as sales because legal title transfer is the sole criterion under ASC 842","Scenario A qualifies as a sale; Scenario B fails because the below-market option prevents control transfer","Scenario A fails sale recognition but Scenario B qualifies since below-market options transfer control","Neither scenario qualifies as a sale because any repurchase option disqualifies the transfer"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-40-25-3, sale-leaseback transactions are evaluated using ASC 606 control transfer criteria. In Scenario A, a fair-value repurchase option does not give the seller-lessee an economic advantage, so the buyer-lessor obtains control and the sale is recognized. In Scenario B, the below-market option creates a significant incentive to reacquire, indicating control was not transferred, so it is treated as financing.
Wrong (A): Legal title alone does not determine sale recognition. ASC 842 requires evaluating economic substance through the control transfer framework of ASC 606.
Wrong (C): This reverses the analysis. A below-market option prevents sale recognition; a fair-value option permits it.
Wrong (D): Fair-value repurchase options do not disqualify sale recognition because they provide no economic advantage to the seller-lessee over an open-market purchase.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1076;

-- Q1078: Advanced Lease Transactions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both modifications are treated as new separate contracts under ASC 842 guidelines","Scenario A is a separate contract adding scope at standalone price; Scenario B requires remeasurement","Scenario A requires remeasurement while Scenario B is treated as a separate contract","Neither modification affects the ROU asset because modifications only impact the lease liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-10-25-8, a lease modification is treated as a separate contract if it adds scope (a new right-of-use asset) at a commensurate standalone price. Scenario A meets both criteria, so a new ROU asset is recognized and the original lease is unaffected. Scenario B merely extends the term at below-market rates, so the existing lease is remeasured with an updated liability and ROU asset adjustment.
Wrong (A): ASC 842 distinguishes between modifications that qualify as separate contracts and those requiring remeasurement. Not all changes create new contracts.
Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment. Adding scope at standalone price creates a separate contract; extending the term without new scope requires remeasurement.
Wrong (D): Remeasurement of a lease modification adjusts both the lease liability and the ROU asset, not just the liability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1078;

-- Q1084: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$200,000 gain on the difference between sale price and carrying value","$0 because the fixed-price option causes a failed sale treated as financing","$100,000 partial gain recognized over the leaseback term","$600,000 gain equal to the full sale price received"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-40-25-3 and ASC 606, a fixed-price repurchase option means the buyer-lessor does not obtain control of the asset. The transaction fails to qualify as a sale, so the seller-lessee keeps the asset on its books and records the $600,000 cash received as a financing obligation. No gain is recognized.
Wrong (A): No gain can be recognized because the transaction is not a sale. The asset remains on the seller-lessee''s balance sheet and the proceeds become a financial liability.
Wrong (C): Partial gain recognition applies to successful sale-leasebacks where the leaseback is at off-market terms. A failed sale produces no gain at all.
Wrong (D): Recording the full sale price as a gain would ignore that the transaction does not qualify as a sale and the asset was never derecognized.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1084;

-- Q1085: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Make no adjustment until the renewal option is formally exercised by the lessee","Remeasure the lease liability for the revised 7-year term and adjust the ROU asset accordingly","Record a new separate lease for the 3-year renewal period starting immediately","Adjust only future amortization of the ROU asset without remeasuring the lease liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842-10-35-1, when a significant event makes it reasonably certain a renewal option will be exercised, the lessee remeasures the lease liability using the revised remaining term (4 + 3 = 7 years) at the current discount rate and adjusts the right-of-use asset accordingly. This reassessment is triggered unilaterally by changed circumstances.
Wrong (A): ASC 842 requires reassessment when reasonable certainty changes, not deferral until formal exercise. Waiting would misstate the liability.
Wrong (C): A reassessment is not a new lease. The existing lease is remeasured to reflect the revised term, maintaining it as a single arrangement.
Wrong (D): Both the lease liability and the ROU asset must be adjusted. Changing only amortization without remeasuring the liability would create a mismatch between the two.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1085;

-- Q1086: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The $500,000 loss has no balance sheet effect because actuarial items are footnote disclosures only","The funded status worsens to $2,000,000 and the loss is recognized in OCI for corridor amortization","The $500,000 is immediately recognized as pension expense, increasing the current period charge","The loss reduces plan assets but does not change funded status because gains and losses offset"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715, the funded status (PBO minus plan assets) is reported on the balance sheet. The $500,000 actuarial loss reduces plan assets from $10,500,000 to $10,000,000, increasing the funded status liability from $1,500,000 to $2,000,000. The loss is recognized in OCI and subsequently amortized into pension expense through the corridor approach.
Wrong (A): The funded status on the balance sheet changes immediately when an actuarial loss occurs. ASC 715 requires full funded status recognition, not just footnote disclosure.
Wrong (C): ASC 715 explicitly avoids immediate income statement recognition for actuarial items. OCI serves as a smoothing mechanism to prevent income volatility from estimation fluctuations.
Wrong (D): The funded status does change because the loss reduces plan assets while the PBO remains the same. Gains and losses do not automatically offset each other.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1086;

-- Q1088: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both plans produce identical accounting because GAAP does not distinguish between plan types","Defined contribution expense equals the contribution with no liability; defined benefit requires actuarial calculations","Defined contribution plans are more complex because employers must track individual participant accounts","Defined benefit plans have simpler accounting because the fixed formula eliminates estimation uncertainty"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715, Harrington''s defined contribution expense equals its annual contribution with no ongoing balance sheet liability, because the obligation is satisfied upon contribution and investment risk transfers to employees. Kelton''s defined benefit plan requires estimating the PBO using actuarial assumptions, recognizing funded status on the balance sheet, and calculating multi-component pension expense.
Wrong (A): ASC 715 establishes fundamentally different accounting treatments for defined contribution and defined benefit plans based on who bears the investment and longevity risk.
Wrong (C): Individual account tracking is an administrative task, not accounting complexity. The defined benefit plan is more complex in its accounting requirements.
Wrong (D): The benefit promise in a defined benefit plan creates estimation uncertainty, not certainty. Actuarial assumptions about discount rates, salary growth, and mortality introduce significant complexity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1088;

-- Q1089: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The discount rate decrease reduces the PBO because lower rates mean smaller future obligations","The discount rate change has no PBO effect because PBO depends on salary projections, not rates","The $2,800,000 increase is immediately recognized as pension expense in the current period","A lower discount rate increases the PBO because present value of future payments rises at lower rates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 715, the PBO is the present value of estimated future benefit payments discounted at high-quality corporate bond yields. When the discount rate decreases from 5% to 4%, the same future payments have a higher present value, increasing the PBO by $2,800,000. This actuarial loss is recognized in OCI and amortized into pension expense via the corridor approach.
Wrong (A): This reverses the inverse relationship between discount rates and present values. Lower discount rates increase, not decrease, the present value of future obligations.
Wrong (B): The discount rate is a primary driver of PBO magnitude. While salary projections also affect the PBO, changes in the discount rate directly alter the present value calculation.
Wrong (C): ASC 715 avoids immediate income statement recognition for actuarial items. The $2,800,000 loss flows through OCI, not pension expense.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1089;

-- Q1093: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The $3,600,000 is recognized as pension expense immediately in the amendment year","The amendment is disclosed only in footnotes with no financial statement recognition","The $3,600,000 is recognized in OCI and amortized into expense over the 12-year service period","The $3,600,000 reduces plan assets because the amendment requires immediate full funding"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715, a plan amendment granting retroactive benefit increases creates prior service cost equal to the $3,600,000 PBO increase. This is recognized in OCI at the amendment date, then amortized into pension expense at $300,000 per year over the 12-year average remaining service period, matching the cost to periods of expected future employee service.
Wrong (A): Immediate full recognition violates the matching principle. The retroactive increase motivates future service, so ASC 715 spreads the cost over the remaining service period.
Wrong (B): The amendment creates real financial statement recognition in OCI and affects the balance sheet funded status. It is not merely a footnote disclosure.
Wrong (D): The amendment changes the PBO, not plan assets. Accounting recognition and funding requirements are separate; the employer may or may not fund the increase immediately.$EXPL$
