-- Q1190: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The $450,000 is recognized as revenue on both statements because taxes were levied and are collectible","The $450,000 is excluded from both statements because uncollected taxes are not reported until received","Fund statements defer the $450,000 as unavailable; government-wide statements recognize it as revenue","Government-wide statements defer the $450,000 while fund statements recognize the full revenue amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under modified accrual accounting, revenue must be measurable and available. The $450,000 expected within 90 days exceeds the 60-day availability period, so it is reported as a deferred inflow on fund statements. On government-wide statements using full accrual, revenue is recognized at the levy date when the enforceable claim arises, so the full $450,000 is revenue.
Wrong (A): The availability criterion prevents fund-level recognition. Although the taxes are measurable, they are not available within the 60-day window.
Wrong (B): Modified accrual does recognize the receivable; it just defers the revenue recognition. The amount is reported as a deferred inflow, not excluded entirely.
Wrong (D): This reverses the treatment. Fund statements apply the availability criterion (deferring revenue); government-wide statements recognize revenue at levy.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1190;

-- Q1191: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both statements treat both payments as expenses in the period paid with no reconciling difference","Principal is an expenditure at fund level but a liability reduction at government-wide; interest is expense on both","The principal payment is capitalized on both statements as an investment in reducing obligations","Interest is the only reconciling item because principal payments are treated identically on both"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the current financial resources focus, both the $2,000,000 principal and $600,000 interest consume current resources and are reported as expenditures. Under the economic resources focus (government-wide), the principal payment reduces bonds payable rather than creating an expense, while interest remains an expense. The reconciliation removes the principal expenditure and records a liability reduction.
Wrong (A): The measurement focus distinction causes principal to be treated fundamentally differently at the two levels. Interest is an expense on both, but principal is not.
Wrong (C): Debt repayment is not capitalized. At the government-wide level, it reduces a liability; at the fund level, it is an expenditure.
Wrong (D): Principal is the primary reconciling item, not interest. Interest is an expense under both measurement focuses and requires no reconciling adjustment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1191;

-- Q1193: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both amounts are subtracted because internal service funds are proprietary funds","Net position is added to the balance sheet reconciliation; change in net position is added to activities","Only the $850,000 net position is added; the $120,000 change is eliminated entirely","Internal service funds are excluded from both reconciliations to governmental activities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 62, internal service funds predominantly serve governmental activities and are consolidated into governmental activities at the government-wide level. The $850,000 net position is added in the balance sheet reconciliation, and the $120,000 change in net position is added in the activities statement reconciliation.
Wrong (A): Although internal service funds are proprietary funds, their amounts are added, not subtracted, because they are consolidated into governmental activities at the government-wide level.
Wrong (C): Both amounts must be included in their respective reconciliations. Excluding the change in net position would leave the activities statement reconciliation incomplete.
Wrong (D): Internal service funds are included because their primary customers are governmental departments. Excluding them would understate governmental activities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1193;

-- Q1194: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Net position = $9,850,000: add $22.8M capital assets, subtract $18M debt, add $850K internal service","Net position = $4,200,000 because the capital asset and debt adjustments cancel each other out","Net position = $40,650,000 because all items are added to show total government resources","Net position = $3,350,000 because capital assets and internal service funds are subtracted from fund balance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34, starting with fund balance of $4,200,000: add capital assets of $22,800,000 (economic resources excluded from funds), subtract long-term debt of $18,000,000 (obligations excluded from funds), and add internal service fund net position of $850,000 (consolidated into governmental activities). The result is $9,850,000.
Wrong (B): Capital assets ($22.8M) and long-term debt ($18M) do not cancel out. The net effect is a $4.8M increase plus the $850K internal service fund addition.
Wrong (C): Long-term debt must be subtracted, not added. Adding all items ignores that debt is an obligation reducing net position.
Wrong (D): Capital assets and internal service fund net position are added, not subtracted. Both represent economic resources that increase government-wide net position.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1194;

-- Q1197: Interfund Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The transfer is eliminated because GASB considers all interfund transfers to be accounting errors","The transfer is reported as program revenue of the public safety function at the government-wide level","The transfer appears at fund level but is eliminated at government-wide as an internal reallocation","The transfer is eliminated at fund level but reported at government-wide to show all cash movements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, interfund transfers between two governmental funds are reported as other financing sources/uses at the fund level. At the government-wide level, both funds roll into governmental activities, so the transfer is an internal reallocation that does not change total net position and is eliminated to avoid double-counting.
Wrong (A): Interfund transfers are legitimate transactions that serve valid budgetary purposes. They are not accounting errors but are eliminated at the government-wide level because they are internal.
Wrong (B): Internal transfers are not program revenues. Program revenues require an external exchange, and this transfer is an internal resource reallocation.
Wrong (D): This reverses the reporting. Transfers appear at the fund level and are eliminated at the government-wide level, not the other way around.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1197;

-- Q1199: Interfund Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both structures produce identical results because GASB treats loans and transfers the same","The loan preserves fund balance via a receivable; the transfer reduces fund balance permanently","The transfer preserves fund balance because transfers are temporary; loans permanently reduce it","Neither loans nor transfers affect fund balance because interfund transactions are always eliminated"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, an interfund loan creates an asset (due from) in the lending fund and a liability (due to) in the borrowing fund, preserving the General Fund''s fund balance. A transfer reduces fund balance by $300,000 with no expectation of repayment. At the government-wide level, the loan appears as an internal balance while the cross-activity transfer is reported.
Wrong (A): Loans and transfers have fundamentally different effects on fund balance and government-wide presentation. The choice of structure materially affects reporting.
Wrong (C): This reverses the analysis. Transfers permanently reduce fund balance; loans preserve it through the receivable asset.
Wrong (D): Interfund balances and cross-activity transfers are reported in financial statements. They are not universally eliminated.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1199;

-- Q1305: Financial Valuation Methods [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Are identical methods governed by the same accounting standards","Use similar techniques but operate in different contexts and under different standards","Cannot both use the income approach for measuring asset values","Are mutually exclusive methods that should never be compared or cross-referenced"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Both ASC 820 fair value measurement and business valuation DCF analysis use income-approach techniques (discounting expected cash flows), but they serve different purposes. ASC 820 measures fair value for financial reporting using market-participant assumptions, while business valuation DCF supports investment decisions and M&A analysis.
Wrong (A): Although the techniques overlap, the standards, assumptions, and purposes differ. ASC 820 governs financial reporting; business valuation follows professional appraisal standards.
Wrong (C): Both methods can and do use the income approach. The income approach is central to both ASC 820 Level 3 measurements and business valuation DCF models.
Wrong (D): The methods are not mutually exclusive. Practitioners regularly compare results from both approaches, and understanding their differences strengthens the analysis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1305;

-- Q2935: Financial Statement Analysis [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reduced accounts payable increased both ratios equally, so the divergence is a calculation error","Improved cash position raised the current ratio while receivable write-offs lowered the quick ratio","A shift from FIFO to LIFO inventory accounting always causes these ratios to move oppositely","Excess inventory buildup inflated current assets while liquid assets like cash and receivables declined"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The current ratio includes inventory while the quick ratio excludes it per ASC 330-10-35. When the current ratio rises while the quick ratio falls with flat revenue, the most likely cause is significant inventory accumulation paired with declining cash or receivables. This signals potential demand weakness or overproduction rather than improving liquidity.
Wrong (A): Reducing accounts payable would increase both ratios simultaneously since it lowers the denominator. It cannot explain opposite movements in the two ratios.
Wrong (B): While receivable write-offs could lower the quick ratio, they would also lower the current ratio since receivables are included in both. This does not explain the current ratio improvement.
Wrong (C): An inventory method change might affect reported inventory value but does not inherently cause opposite ratio movements. The divergence is better explained by inventory quantity changes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2935;
